From b9ee2e8da759fcaa2171f0066e80e38c034f892b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:19:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Each display keeps the mode of its menus

Kodi keeps one mode of the menus (videoscreen.screenmode), of whatever display it was set on, and a
start or a move of Kodi to another display takes it there where that display has the mode. An
LG C5 started in the 2560x1440 at 120 Hz that Kodi had on the Samsung
Odyssey G60SD before; Kodi's default whitelist does not leave the resolution of the menus for a video
of 1920x1080, so a film went at 119.998 Hz there instead of the LG C5's 3840x2160 at 23.976 Hz.

- The mode of the menus of each display, by the make and model of its EDID, is kept in
  special://profile/hades-display-modes.txt (DisplayModeMemory.h): noted whenever the mode of the
  settings is set while that display shows Kodi (CDisplaySettings::OnSettingChanged() ->
  CWinSystemBase::ScreenModeChosen()).
- CDisplaySettings::GetDisplayResolution(), which a start, a move of Kodi to another output
  (SetResolutionAfterMove()) and a change of the display's modes take, asks the window system for
  the mode of the display in use (ScreenModeFor()): the one it had; for a display new to Kodi the mode
  it prefers (DESKTOP). The settings are then made that mode, with no question (the resolution set is
  the current one already): at a start once the window is up, after a move at once.
- Kodi on one display only: the mode of the settings, as always, noted for that display (the first
  start with this build notes the display in use). A display that Kodi cannot name (no EDID): as
  always. kodi.log says when the menus of a display go in another mode than that of the settings.

Checked: a host test (display_modes_test.cpp: the G60SD and then the LG C5, back and forth, a new
display, one display, the file read back, the limit of 32 displays) with two mutants; the changed
files and the sources that include them compile with the commands of the image's build. Not run on a
GPU.
